UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 1: Role and character', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCreate a character who is a nervous new student. Choose two traits and say how you would show them with your body, face and voice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It links each trait to a physical choice, which makes a character believable.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can believe in them.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake three characters: a king, a robot and a shy mouse. For each write two traits, a way of standing and moving, a face and a voice. Perform one for a family member who guesses the traits. You may describe instead of performing.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make three characters: a king, a robot and a shy mouse. For each write two traits, a way of standing and moving, a face and a voice. Perform one for a family member who guesses the traits. You may describe instead of per Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It links each trait to a physical choice,… Reasoning: I used a role by attending to a character rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can… Correction: Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a role.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can believe in them. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a role on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a role, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It… This matches the lesson skill for a role.","options":["Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It…","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Create a character who is a nervous new student. Choose two traits and say how you would show them with your body, face and voice. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a character with clear evidence.","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…","In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants.…","Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a character, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a character used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a role. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It…","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a role."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a character?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low,…","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave,… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can…","Make three characters: a king, a robot and a shy mouse. For each write two traits, a way of standing and moving, a face and a voice. Perform one for…","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a role on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It links each trait to a physical choice,… Reasoning: I used a role by attending to a character rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a role, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can… Correction: Strong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a role.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w11-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a role.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use a role with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a character and explain why it matters.","I can practise a character trait independently on a new prompt.","I can notice staying in role needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low, slow voice and sighs. Her character wants to catch one last fish. Everyone can tell the character''s traits from her body and voice, and she keeps in role until the teacher says ''freeze and thank you''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCreate a character who is a nervous new student. Choose two traits and say how you would show them with your body, face and voice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It links each trait to a physical choice, which makes a character believable.","explain":"In drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can believe in them. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A character trait shown by the body.","caption":"Show the trait.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Trait</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">shy, worried</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Body</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">hunched, looks down</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, sp Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, sp Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low, slow voice and sighs. Her character wants to catch one last fish. Everyone can tell the character''s traits from her body and voice, and she keeps in role until the teacher says ''freeze and thank you''. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Voice and face show a character.","caption":"Voice and face help.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Voice</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">quiet, shaky</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Face</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wide eyes</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can believe in them. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low, slow voice and sighs. Her character wants to catch one last fish. Everyone can tell the character''s traits from her body and voice, and she keeps in role until the teacher says ''freeze and thank you''.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Traits: shy and worried. Body: hunched shoulders, looking at the floor. Face: eyes wide, small smile. Voice: quiet and a little shaky.'' It links each trait to a physical choice, which makes a character believable.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that drama means just acting silly or copying someone from TV. Acting means making deliberate choices about a character''s traits so the audience can believe in them. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a role?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn drama, a role is a person or creature you pretend to be. A character is built from traits, such as brave, shy or grumpy, and from what the character wants. You show a character through your body (how you stand and move), your face and your voice. Staying in role means you keep acting as the character until the teacher or leader says to stop. Drama is pretend and safe, with no hitting or real touching. You can always step out of a role or describe it if you do not want to act. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 11, Lesson 1: Role and character","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":11,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Zoe plays an old fisherman. She decides his traits are patient and tired. She walks slowly with a bent back, speaks in a low, slow voice and sighs. Her character wants to catch one last fish. Everyone can tell the character''s traits from her body and voice, and she keeps in role until the teacher says ''freeze and thank you''.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A character trait shown by the body.","caption":"Show the trait.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Trait</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">shy, worried</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Body</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">hunched, looks down</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Voice and face show a character.","caption":"Voice and face help.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Voice</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">quiet, shaky</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Face</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wide eyes</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w11-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
